A food web in a grassland (illustrative) is described by these arrows, each pointing from the food to the eater:
grass -> grasshopper; grass -> mouse; grass -> hare;
grasshopper -> frog; grasshopper -> bird;
frog -> snake; mouse -> snake; mouse -> hawk;
bird -> hawk; snake -> hawk; hare -> hawk.
(a) How many different food chains run from grass to the hawk?
(b) Write the longest of these chains.
(c) At which trophic levels does the hawk feed in this web?

On a walk with their teacher through a village in Haryana, students noted:
(i) honeybees visiting mustard flowers;
(ii) a fern growing on the trunk of a neem tree, which looks healthy;
(iii) ticks on a stray dog that keeps scratching;
(iv) two kinds of birds chasing each other away from the same fig tree full of fruit.
(a) Name the relationship in each of (i), (ii) and (iii).
(b) Which observation shows the kind of interaction that the chapter says helps control population size?
(c) In (iii), state who benefits and how, and who is harmed and how.
(d) A mustard farmer must choose before flowering (illustrative figures). If bees visit, 80 of every 100 flowers set seed; with no insects, only 35 of every 100 do. Leaf-eating insects would destroy about 10% of the crop if left alone. Plan A: keep beehives and do not spray. Plan B: spray a pesticide that kills every insect on the plants while they flower. Using observation (i), decide which plan gives more seed.
